The '''Jaguar XJ220''' is a two-seat [[supercar]] produced by British luxury car manufacturer [[Jaguar Cars|Jaguar]] from 1992 until 1994, in collaboration with the specialist automotive and race engineering company [[Tom Walkinshaw Racing]].{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=42}}<ref name='JaguarPR'>{{cite news |title=Marking 20 Years Since The Launch of the Jaguar XJ220 |date=30 January 2012 |publisher=[[Jaguar Land Rover]] |url=http://media.jaguar.com/news/2012/01/marking-20-years-launch-jaguar-xj220 |work=Jaguar Land Rover Newsroom |access-date=24 June 2013 |archive-date=17 February 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170217062706/http://media.jaguar.com/news/2012/01/marking-20-years-launch-jaguar-xj220 |url-status=live }}</ref> The XJ220 (with catalytic converter removed) recorded a top speed of {{cvt|217|mph|kph}} during testing by Jaguar at the Nardo test track in Italy.<ref name="Car 1992">{{cite news |last=Green |first=Gavin |url=http://media.wix.com/ugd/98b542_6c3dd4c6112d4efd94bfdde470dc4df4.pdf |title=217mph! |work=[[Car (magazine)|Car]] |location=London |date=August 1992 |access-date=18 February 2017 |archive-date=11 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221111155316/http://media.wix.com/ugd/98b542_6c3dd4c6112d4efd94bfdde470dc4df4.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> This made it the [[Production car speed record|fastest production car]] from 1992 to 1993.<ref name="Car Nardo">{{cite news |last=Taylor |first=James |url=http://www.carmagazine.co.uk/features/car-culture/flat-out-for-40-years-happy-birthday-to-the-nardo-test-track/ |title=Flat-out for 40 years: happy birthday to the Nardo test track |work=[[Car (magazine)|Car]] |location=London |date=1 July 2015 |access-date=22 February 2017 |archive-date=23 February 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170223042221/http://www.carmagazine.co.uk/features/car-culture/flat-out-for-40-years-happy-birthday-to-the-nardo-test-track/ |url-status=live }}</ref> According to Jaguar, an XJ220 prototype managed a [[Nürburgring]] lap time of 7:46.36 in 1991 which was faster than any production car lap time before it.<ref name='JaguarPR' /><ref name='Evo2'>{{cite news |first= Richard |last=Meaden |title=Kings of the 'Ring |date=July 2000 |work=[[Evo (magazine)|Evo]] |page=85}}</ref><ref name='OctaneSpec'>{{cite web |url=http://www.classicandperformancecar.com/front_website/octane_interact/carspecs.php/?see=1768 |title=1992–1994 JAGUAR XJ220 |access-date=24 June 2013 |work=[[Octane (magazine)|Octane]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131007032715/http://www.classicandperformancecar.com/front_website/octane_interact/carspecs.php/?see=1768 |archive-date=7 October 2013 }}</ref><ref name='AutoExpress1'>{{cite news |title=Jaguar XJ220 |date=2 February 2010 |url=http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/car-reviews/39429/jaguar-xj220 |work=[[Auto Express]] |access-date=24 June 2013 |archive-date=6 October 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131006170428/http://www.autoexpress.co.uk/car-reviews/39429/jaguar-xj220 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The XJ220 was developed from a V12-engined 4-wheel drive concept car designed by an informal group of Jaguar employees working in their spare time.<ref name='JaguarPR'/> The group wished to create a modern version of the successful Jaguar [[24 Hours of Le Mans]] racing cars of the 1950s and 1960s that could be entered into FIA [[Group B]] competitions.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=29}} The XJ220 made use of engineering work undertaken for Jaguar's then current racing car family.<ref name='BBCTG1'>{{cite web |url=http://www.topgear.com/uk/photos/The-Jaguar-XJ220-turns-20-2012-02-01 |title=The Jaguar XJ220 turns 20 |date=1 February 2012 |website=topgear.com |publisher=BBC Worldwide |access-date=2026-05-13 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150131051237/http://www.topgear.com/uk/photos/The-Jaguar-XJ220-turns-20-2012-02-01 |archive-date=2015-01-31 }}</ref>

The initial XJ220 concept car was unveiled to the public at the 1988 [[British International Motor Show]], held in [[Birmingham]], England.<ref name='JaguarPress1988'>{{cite news |title=Jaguar Unleases XJ220 Supercar |date=18 October 1988 |publisher=[[Jaguar Cars]] |url=http://www.jag-lovers.org/brochures/xj220_88_press.html |work=Jaguar Communications and Public Affairs Department |access-date=25 June 2013 |archive-date=5 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130705060942/http://www.jag-lovers.org/brochures/xj220_88_press.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Its positive reception prompted Jaguar to put the car into production. Approximately 281 deposits of £50,000 each were taken and deliveries were planned for 1992.<ref name='JaguarPR' /><ref name='BBCTG1'/>

Engineering and emissions requirements resulted in significant changes to the specification of the XJ220, most notably the replacement of the [[Jaguar V12 engine]] by a [[turbocharged]] [[V6 engine]]. The changes to the specification and a collapse in the demand of high performance cars brought about by the [[early 1990s recession]] resulted in many buyers choosing not to exercise their purchase options. A total of just 275 cars were produced by the time production ended, each with a retail price of £470,000 in 1992, making it one of the most expensive cars at that time.<ref name='JaguarPR' /><ref name='AutoExpress1' />

==Conception==
Racing team owner [[Tom Walkinshaw]] approached Jaguar executives and encouraged the company to enter the [[Jaguar XJS|XJS]] into the 1981 [[European Touring Car Championship]]. The partnership succeeded in winning the competition in 1983.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=29}}<ref name='PH1'>{{cite news |first=Richard |last=Aucock |title=JAGUAR XJ220&nbsp;– THE INSIDE STORY |date=8 August 2012 |publisher=Haymarket Media Group |url=http://www.pistonheads.com/doc.asp?c=52&i=26143 |work=[[PistonHeads]] |access-date=24 June 2013 |archive-date=1 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130701190339/http://www.pistonheads.com/doc.asp?c=52&i=26143 |url-status=live }}</ref> Jaguar had started to provide factory support to racing team Group 44 Racing, who were using the Jaguar-engined [[Jaguar XJR-5|XJR-5]] in the [[IMSA GT Championship]], supplying V12 engines from 1983 onwards and supporting a Le Mans entry in 1984. Tom Walkinshaw and Jaguar agreed to enter the FIA [[Group C]] [[World Sportscar Championship]] and developed the XJR-6, which was powered by the same Jaguar V12 engine used by Group 44 Racing. The car was launched during the 1985 season.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=29}}<ref name='PH1' />

TWR took over the IMSA GT Championship operation in 1988 and one model&nbsp;– [[Jaguar XJR-9|XJR-9]]&nbsp;– was launched to compete in both series. The XJR-9, which retained the Jaguar V12 engine, went on to win the 1988 24 Hours of Le Mans and World Sportscar Championship in the same year. The poor fuel consumption of the Jaguar V12 combined with new rules restricting refueling during races, forced the replacement of the V12 engine in the successors to the XJR-9, the [[Jaguar XJR-10|XJR-10]] and [[Jaguar XJR-11|XJR-11]]. The normally aspirated Austin Rover V64V engine, designed for the [[Austin Metro#MG Metro 6R4 rally car|MG Metro 6R4]] had recently been made redundant due to the Group B rally ban in 1987 and the design rights were for sale. The compact, lightweight and fuel efficient nature of the small-displacement, turbocharged engine was investigated by TWR. They considered it an ideal basis for a new engine to power the XJR-10 and purchased the design rights from Austin Rover Group.<ref name='PH1' /><ref name="IMSA">{{cite book |last1=Martin |first1=J. A. |last2=Fuller |first2=Michael J. |title=Inside IMSA's Legendary GTP Race Cars |chapter=Group 44 Inc. And Jaguar |date=15 April 2008 |pages=51–52 |publisher=Motorbooks |isbn=978-0760330692 }}</ref>

Jaguar and their Director of Engineering, Jim Randle, felt these racing cars were too far removed from the product available to the general public, especially with the rule changes that mandated the replacement of the Jaguar V12 engine in the forthcoming XJR-10 and XJR-11 racing cars. Therefore, a project was initiated to design and build a car capable of winning Le Mans "in house", just as the [[Jaguar C-Type|C-Type]] and [[Jaguar D-Type|D-Type]] had done.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=29}} The groundwork for the project was undertaken by Randle over Christmas 1987, when he produced a 1:4 scale cardboard model of a potential Group B racing car.<ref name='AutoExpress1' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=29}}<ref name='BBCTG1' /><ref name='PH1' />

The cardboard model was taken into the Jaguar styling studio and two mock-ups were produced. One was said to be reminiscent of the [[Porsche 956]], the other took elements of the then current XJ41 project and [[Malcolm Sayer]]'s work on the stillborn [[Jaguar XJ13|XJ13]] racing car. The second design, by [[Keith Helfet]], was chosen as it was "more obviously Jaguar in its look".<ref name='JaguarPR' /><ref name='PH1' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=30}}

The project still had no official support, leaving Randle no option but to put together a team of volunteers to work evenings and weekends in their own time. The team came to be known as "The Saturday Club" and consisted of twelve volunteers.<ref name='AutoExpress1' /><ref name='BBCTG1' /><ref name='PH1' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=30}} To justify the resources consumed by the project, the XJ220 needed to provide meaningful data to the engineers on handling, aerodynamics&nbsp;– particularly at high speeds&nbsp;– and aluminium structures. These requirements, together with FIA racing regulations and various government regulations governing car design and safety, influenced the overall design and engineering direction of the car.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=31}}
{{clear}}

==Concept car==
[[File:Jaguar XJ220 top Heritage Motor Centre, Gaydon.jpg|thumb|right|Jaguar XJ220 concept car featuring the [[V12 engine]] at the [[British Motor Museum]], Gaydon]]
[[File:Jaguar XJ220 V12 engine Heritage Motor Centre, Gaydon.jpg|thumb|The Four-Valve Jaguar V12 engine in the XJ220 concept]]
The FIA [[Group B]] regulations steered the concept towards a [[mid-engine design|mid-engine]], [[all-wheel-drive]] layout, with a Jaguar [[V12 engine]] as the power source.<ref name='JaguarPress1988' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=31}} The concept car was designed and built at very little cost to Jaguar, as Randle called in favours from component suppliers and engineering companies he and Jaguar had worked with in the past. In return he offered public recognition for their assistance and dangled the possibility of future contracts from Jaguar.<ref name='AutoExpress1' /><ref name='JaguarPress1988' /><ref name='PH1' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=31}}

The name XJ220 was chosen as a continuation of the naming of the [[Jaguar XK120|XK120]], which referred to the top speed of the model in miles per hour. The concept car had a targeted top speed of {{cvt|220|mi/h|km/h}} so the name became XJ220.<ref name='BBCTG1' /> The XJ220, like the XK120, was an aluminium-bodied sports car and when launched was the fastest production car in the world.{{sfnp|Holloway|Buckley|2003|p=}}

===Engine and transmission===
Jaguar's engine designer [[Walter Hassan]] had previously developed a 48-[[poppet valve|valve]] variant of their V12 engine specifically for motorsport use. It featured a [[double overhead camshaft]] layout with four valves per [[cylinder (engine)|cylinder]], compared with the single overhead camshaft and two valves per cylinder of the production engine, which was used in the [[Jaguar XJ|XJ]] and [[Jaguar XJS|XJS]] models at the time.<ref name='JaguarPress1988' /><ref name='PH1' /><ref name='Observer1'>{{cite news |first=Rivers |last=Fletcher |title=Obituary: Walter Hassan |date=17 July 1996 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/obituaries/obituary-walter-hassan-1329134.html |work=[[The Independent]] |access-date=24 June 2013 |archive-date=3 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121103073206/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/obituaries/obituary-walter-hassan-1329134.html |url-status=live }}</ref>{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=33}}

TWR had manufactured a number of these racing V12 engines during the 1980s and they had been raced competitively, with a 7-litre version of this engine featuring in the Le Mans-winning XJR-9. Five of these engines still existed, all of which were fitted with [[dry sump]] lubrication. One of five of these engines was chosen and considered to be especially useful as the dry sump would lower the vehicle's [[Center of mass|centre of gravity]]. The displacement of the V12 was set at 6.2 litres (6,222&nbsp;cc) for the XJ220.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=33}}{{sfnp|Heilig|Adler|Leffingwell|2004|p=85}} The engine fitted to the XJ220 concept had [[titanium]] [[connecting rod]]s.<ref name='JaguarPress1988' />

Jaguar had little experience with four-wheel drive systems at the time, having previously only produced [[rear wheel drive|rear-wheel drive]] cars. Randle approached [[Tony Rolt]]'s company, [[FF Developments]] to design the transmission and four-wheel drive system for the XJ220, with Rolt's son Stuart running the project. Tony Rolt was the Technical Director of [[Ferguson Research]], where he was heavily involved in the design of the four-wheel drive system used in the [[Jensen FF]], the first sports car to be fitted with such a system.{{sfnp|Robson|2006|p=}}<ref name='Rolt1'>{{cite news |first=Alan |last=Henry |title=Obituary: Tony Rolt |date=9 February 2008 |work=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref> Tony Rolt also had a long involvement with Jaguar, winning the [[1953 24 Hours of Le Mans]] with the factory works team driving the C-Type.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=31}}{{sfnp|Heilig|Adler|Leffingwell|2004|p=85}}<ref name='Rolt1' />

The mid-mounted engine complicated the design of the four-wheel drive system and an innovative solution was needed to get power from the mid-mounted rear engine to the front wheels. The chosen design took the front-wheel drive from the central differential on the rear [[transaxle]] and sent it through the V in the centre of the engine using a [[quill drive]], before joining an inverted [[differential (mechanical device)|differential]].{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=31}} The clutch was a twin-plate unit designed by [[AP Racing]].<ref name='PH1' />

===Bodywork and interior===
The design brief for the exterior restricted the use of aerodynamic aids and aimed for a simple yet clean and functional body similar to classic Jaguar sports cars, such as the D-Type and E-Type.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=33}} Drag and lift were limited at the envisioned ground clearance for road use, but the design allowed for additional downforce when the car was set up for racing. The body produced around {{cvt|3000|lb|kg|}} of downforce at {{cvt|200|mph|kph|0}}. The design was also intended to have a variable rear wing that folded into the bodywork at lower speeds.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=33}} Aerodynamic work was undertaken at the [[MIRA Ltd.|Motor Industry Research Association]] wind tunnel using a 1:4 scale model, as the project was unable to budget for a full-scale mock-up.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=33}}

The bodywork for the concept car displayed in 1988 was hand built from [[aluminium]] by Park Sheet Metal, a specialist automotive engineering company that manufactures concept cars and low-volume niche models for various manufacturers, including [[Bentley]]. QCR Motors LTD undertook final painting of the body in silver.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=37}} The concept also featured electrically operated [[scissor doors]] and a transparent engine cover to show off the V12 engine.<ref name='JaguarPress1988' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=33}}

The concept car had a [[Connolly Leather]]-trimmed interior trimmed by Callow & Maddox and was fitted with front and rear [[defogger|heated windscreens]], [[power window|electric windows]], [[air conditioning]], heated electrically adjustable [[power seat|seats]] and an [[Alpine Electronics|Alpine]] CD player. The dashboard was supplied by Veglia.<ref name='JaguarPress1988' />

===Chassis===
The chassis was manufactured from aluminium using [[Alcan]]'s bonded aluminium structure vehicle technology (ASVT) and had a wheelbase of {{cvt|2845|mm|in|0}}.<ref name='JaguarPress1988' /> The design for the chassis featured [[Steering#Four-wheel steering|rear wheel steering]] and packaged the fuel tank behind the centre bulkhead.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=34}} Suspension design largely focused on road use, but a good compromise for racing use was achieved and the suspension height was adjustable.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=34}} The concept car was fitted with a four-channel [[anti-lock braking system]].<ref name='JaguarPress1988' />

The concept car was larger than the production model at {{cvt|5140|mm|in|}} in length and was {{cvt|2000|mm|in|}} wide. It weighed {{cvt|1560|kg|lb|0}}.<ref name='JaguarPress1988' />

===Launch===
The concept car was completed in the early hours of 18 October 1988, the day it was due to be unveiled at the [[British International Motor Show]], being held at the [[National Exhibition Centre]], [[Birmingham]]. The vehicle was completed at 03:00 GMT, moved to Jaguar's stand at 06:00 GMT and unveiled at 11:00 GMT.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=38}}

Jaguar's marketing department had allocated space on their stand at the motor show for the XJ220, but had not seen the vehicle until its arrival. Jaguar chairman [[John Egan (industrialist)|John Egan]] and Roger Putnam, who was in charge of Jaguar's racing activities, were shown the vehicle the week before the motor show and signed off on the concept, allowing its unveiling.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=7}} The car received an overwhelmingly positive reception by public and press and a number of wealthy Jaguar enthusiasts handed over blank cheques to secure a purchase option should the XJ220 concept go into production. [[Ferrari]]'s display of their [[Ferrari F40|F40]] flagship model at the same event was overshadowed, as an estimated 90,000 additional visitors came to see the XJ220.<ref name='PH1' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=38}}

The XJ220 was not initially intended to be a production car, but following the reception of the concept and financial interest from serious buyers, a feasibility study was carried out by teams from TWR and Jaguar. The conclusion was that such a car would be technically feasible (subject to engineering changes) and that it would be financially viable.<ref name='PH1' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=38}} The announcement of a limited production run of 220 to 350 cars was made on 20 December 1989. The list price on 1 January 1990 was £290,000 exclusive of [[value-added tax]] (VAT), options and delivery charges, but by 1992 that had increased considerably owing to [[indexation of contracts]]. Deposits of £50,000 exclusive of (VAT) were taken from around 281 customers. First deliveries were planned for mid-1992.<ref name='XJ220Order'>{{cite news |title=Jaguar XJ220 Application Form&nbsp;– Terms and Conditions of Order |year=1990 |publisher=Project XJ220 |url=http://www.jag-lovers.org/brochures/xj220order.html |work=XJ220 Application Form |access-date=25 June 2013 |archive-date=4 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130704011203/http://www.jag-lovers.org/brochures/xj220order.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
{{clear}}

==Production version==
[[File:JaguarXJR10-3paddock-91wpb.jpg|thumb|The XJ220 used a road-legal version of the turbocharged V6 racing engine used in the XJR-10 (pictured) and XJR-11 race cars]]

Jaguar were unable to develop the XJ220 in-house as the available engineering resources were committed to working on the XJ and XJS models. Jaguar and TWR had an existing joint venture, JaguarSport Ltd. formed in 1987 to produce racing cars. Jaguar's board made the decision that subject to contractual agreement, TWR and JaguarSport would be responsible for the XJ220. JaguarSport formed a new company, Project XJ220 Ltd., specifically to develop and build the XJ220.<ref name='XJ220Order' />

[[File:1994 Jaguar XJ220 (14969266456).jpg|left|thumb|Jaguar XJ220 with aftermarket wheels]]

The team that would determine the necessary engineering work and assess the car's financial viability was put in place during mid-1989, working from the TWR workshops. Mike Moreton headed the team, joining TWR to run the XJ220 project. Moreton came from [[Ford Motorsport]] where he led the team responsible for the [[Ford Sierra RS Cosworth#Sierra RS500 Cosworth|Ford Sierra RS500 Cosworth]], and was a project manager for the [[Ford RS200]] Group B rally car program.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=41}} Richard Owen was appointed chief designer and the remainder of the team was made up of Jaguar and TWR staff, including Pete Dodd, the only member of the group of twelve responsible for the XJ220 concept. The exterior and interior designers who had worked on the XJ220 prototype, Keith Helfet and Nick Hull, rejoined the project when it became clear that more design work would be needed.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=52}}

===Development===
The development team looked at the two principal competitors, the [[Ferrari F40]] and the [[Porsche 959]]. These were powered by compact, lightweight engines; both the Ferrari and the Porsche used [[forced induction]] to obtain high power outputs from small-displacement engines. Ferrari used a {{convert|2.9|L|cuin|adj=on}} [[twin-turbocharged]] [[V8 engine]] that was rated at {{cvt|478|PS|kW hp|0|order=flip}} whilst Porsche used a {{convert|2.9|L|cuin|adj=on}} twin-turbocharged [[flat-six engine]] rated at {{cvt|450|PS|kW hp|0|order=flip}}, resulting in cars that were significantly lighter and smaller than the XJ220 concept: the Ferrari was lighter by {{cvt|600|kg|lb|0}} and {{cvt|710|mm|in|0}} shorter, whilst the Porsche was {{cvt|250|kg|lb|0}} lighter and {{cvt|870|mm|in|0}} shorter.<ref name='FerrariF40-1'>{{cite web |url=http://www.ferrari.com/english/gt_sport%20cars/classiche/all_models/Pages/F40.aspx |title=F40 |access-date=25 June 2013 |author=Ferrari |author-link=Ferrari |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111001042235/http://www.ferrari.com/English/GT_Sport%20Cars/Classiche/All_Models/Pages/F40.aspx |archive-date=1 October 2011 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name='Porsche959-1'>{{cite news |first=Patrick |last=Hong |title=Extreme Machines |date=1 December 2002 |url=http://www.roadandtrack.com/car-reviews/car-comparison-tests/extreme-machines-porsche-959 |work=[[Road & Track]] |access-date= 5 June 2013}}</ref>{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=45}} The Porsche's specifications were closer to the Jaguar's, with [[all-wheel drive]] and a luxurious interior. By comparison, the rear-wheel drive Ferrari had a very basic interior, with no carpets, door handles or a stereo.<ref name='FerrariF40-1' /><ref name='Porsche959-1' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=45}}

===Engine===
[[File:XJ220 Engine view.jpg|thumb|The production XJ220's V6 engine is visible through the rear window]]

The production version of the XJ220 used a {{cvt|3498|cc|L CID|1}} [[twin-turbocharged]] [[V6 engine]], which was given the designation ''Jaguar/TWR JV6'' [[Jaguar JRV-6 engine]] . This engine, which replaced the [[Jaguar V12 engine]] featured in the concept car, was a heavily redesigned and significantly altered version of the Austin Rover V64V V6 engine.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /><ref name='JaguarPR' /><ref name='AutoExpress1' /><ref name='PH1' /><ref name="IMSA" />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=45}}<ref name='EVOMG1'>{{cite news |first=Ian |last=Eveleigh |title=Birth of an icon: MG Metro 6R4 |date=May 2010 |url=http://www.evo.co.uk/features/features/260041/birth_of_an_icon_mg_metro_6r4.html |work=[[Evo (magazine)|Evo]] |access-date=24 June 2013 |archive-date=28 May 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140528134917/http://www.evo.co.uk/features/features/260041/birth_of_an_icon_mg_metro_6r4.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The decision to change the engine was based on engine weight and dimensions, as well as [[Vehicle emissions control|environmental emission]] considerations. Use of the shorter V6 engine design allowed the wheelbase of the XJ220 to be shortened and its weight to be reduced; the V12 engine was definitively ruled out when it was determined it would have difficulty at simultaneously meeting emissions legislations.<ref name='BBCTG1' />

TWR purchased the rights to the V64V engine from Austin Rover in 1989 and developed a completely new turbocharged engine, codenamed JV6, under the auspices of Allan Scott, with proportions roughly similar to the V64V, and suitable for sports car racing.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.motorsportmagazine.com/archive/article/august-1990/33/jaguar-plans|title=Jaguar Plans – Motor Sport Magazine Archive|date=7 July 2014|access-date=2 November 2018|archive-date=16 December 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181216032019/https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/archive/article/august-1990/33/jaguar-plans|url-status=live}}</ref> As the V64V was originally naturally aspirated, it was necessary to redesign all parts of the engine to accommodate forced induction. A few of the changes included increasing the displacement to 3.5 litres, strengthening the internals and adding two [[Garrett AiResearch|Garrett]] TO3 turbochargers. The JV6 engine would first be used in the JaguarSport XJR-10 and XJR-11 racing cars; its compact dimensions and low weight made it an ideal candidate for the XJ220.<ref name='EVOMG1' /> The engine had a 90° bank angle, four valves per cylinder and belt-driven [[double overhead camshaft]]s.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /><ref name='EVOMG1' /><ref name='V64V1'>{{cite news |url=http://www.6r4.net/site/6r4-history/the-history-of-the-6r4 |first=David |last=Sim |title=The history of the 6R4 |year=2011 |work=6R4.net |access-date=27 February 2020 |archive-date=28 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200228035553/http://www.6r4.net/site/6r4-history/the-history-of-the-6r4 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The V64V engine chosen had a short but successful career as a purpose-designed racing car engine.<ref name='V64V1' /> It was designed by [[Cosworth]] engine designer David Wood for Austin Rover Group's [[Austin Metro|Metro]] derived [[Group B]] rally car, the [[MG Metro 6R4]].<ref name='V64V1' /> The redesign work necessary to create the Jaguar/TWR JV6 engine was undertaken by Andrew Barnes, TWR's Powertrain Manager, and also involved Swiss engine builder Max Heidegger, who had designed and built the race engines used in the XJR-10 and XJR-11 racing cars.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=57}}{{sfnp|Porter|1994|p=126}}

The XJ220's engine had a bore x stroke of {{cvt|94|x|84|mm|2}}, dry sump lubrication, [[Fuel injection#Multi-point injection|multi point fuel injection]] with dual injectors and [[Zytek]] [[Engine control unit|electronic engine management]]. The engine was manufactured with an aluminium cylinder block and aluminium [[cylinder head]]s with steel connecting rods and crankshaft. In the standard state of tune, it was rated at a power output of {{cvt|550|PS|kW hp|0}} at 7,200&nbsp;rpm and torque of {{cvt|475|lbft|Nm|}} at 4,500&nbsp;rpm. The XJ220 could accelerate from 0–{{cvt|60|mph}} in 3.6 seconds and could attain a top speed of {{cvt|212.3|mph}}. ''[[Road & Track]]'' tested a top speed of {{cvt|210.5|mph|km/h}}, 0–{{cvt|60|mph|km/h}} acceleration in 4.8 seconds and 12.4 seconds for the standing {{convert|1/4|mi|m|0}}.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /><ref name='OctaneSpec' /><ref name='PH1' /><ref name="RT95">[[Road & Track]] January 1995 p.63-71</ref>

The XJ220's exhaust system consists of two [[catalytic converter]]s, which reduce the power output of the engine. During testing at the [[Nardò Ring]] in Italy, one XJ220 had its catalytic converters removed and its rev limit increased to 7,900&nbsp;rpm; these modifications increased the car's power output by {{cvt|51|hp|kW PS|0}} and the car was then driven by [[1990 24 Hours of Le Mans|1990 Le Mans Winner]] [[Martin Brundle]] to a top speed of {{cvt|217.1|mph|0}}. Owing to the circular nature of the track, some (including the British car magazines ''[[Top Gear (magazine)|Top Gear]]'' and ''[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]]'') say a speed of {{cvt|217|mph|kph|0}} is equivalent to {{cvt|223|mph|km/h|0}} on a straight, level road. However, on other tracks it performed differently; when top speed was tested on a straight level road by ''[[Road & Track]]'' it only amounted to {{cvt|210.5|mph|0}}, it was slower than the [[Bugatti EB 110]] at the same event and the [[Ruf CTR]] tested there in 1987.<ref name='BBCTG1' /><ref name="RT95"/><ref name='COYS1'>{{cite web |url=http://www.coys.co.uk/jaguar_xj220.php |title=1994 Jaguar XJ220 |access-date=24 June 2013 |author=Coy's Auctioneers |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140419014654/http://www.coys.co.uk/jaguar_xj220.php |archive-date=19 April 2014 |df=dmy-all }}</ref><ref name='JaguarPress1992'>{{cite news |title=The Jaguar XJ220 |date=August 1992 |publisher=[[Jaguar Cars]] |url=http://www.jag-lovers.org/brochures/xj220_press_892.html |work=Jaguar Communications and Public Affairs Department |access-date=25 June 2013 |archive-date=4 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130704213402/http://www.jag-lovers.org/brochures/xj220_press_892.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="RT87">{{cite journal |url=http://www.roadandtrack.com/car-culture/a29347/the-worlds-fastest-cars-1987/ |title=In 1987, The World's Fastest Cars Couldn't Catch A 211-mph Twin-Turbo Ruf |first=Peter |last=Egan |journal=[[Road & Track]] |location=US |date=2016-05-29 |access-date=2017-02-14 |archive-date=2 August 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190802212854/https://www.roadandtrack.com/car-culture/a29347/the-worlds-fastest-cars-1987/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The V64V engine had the additional benefit of being very economical for such a powerful [[petrol engine]], according to Jaguar it was capable of achieving {{cvt|32|mpgimp}}. In contrast, the Jaguar saloon having the smallest engine of the time, the [[Jaguar XJ (XJ40)#XJ6 (1986–1994)|XJ6 3.2]] could only achieve around {{cvt|24|mpgimp}}.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' />

The engine was manufactured and built at the new purpose-built facility in [[Kidlington, Oxfordshire]]. [[Tom Walkinshaw Racing|TWR]] invested heavily in a state of the art machining facility capable of servicing all of its race and road car projects. The facility was initially setup to produce the XJ220's engine under Simon Lockwood, an ex-March Engineering suspension specialist.

===Transmission===
All-wheel drive was decided against early in the development process, for a number of reasons. It was thought rear-wheel drive would be adequate in the majority of situations, that the additional complexity of the four-wheel drive system would hinder the development process and potentially be problematic for the customer.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=47}} FF Developments were contracted to provide the [[gearbox]]/[[transaxle]] assembly, modifying their all-wheel drive transaxle assembly from the XJ220 concept into a pure [[rear-wheel drive]] design for the production car.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=57}} The transaxle featured a [[Limited-slip differential#Viscous differential|viscous coupling limited slip differential]] to improve traction.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' />

The transmission featured triple-cone synchromeshing on first and second gears to handle rapid starts, whilst remaining relatively easy for the driver to engage and providing positive feel.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /><ref name='FFGearbox'>{{cite journal |title=The Jaguar XJ220 Triple-Cone Synchronizer A Case Study |journal=SAE Technical Papers |date=3 January 1994 |first=Lee M. |last=Sykes |series=SAE Technical Paper Series |volume=1 |article-number=940737 |doi=10.4271/940737 |url=http://papers.sae.org/940737/ |access-date=26 June 2013 |archive-date=21 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150621115123/http://papers.sae.org/940737/ |url-status=live |url-access=subscription }}</ref>

AP Racing provided an {{cvt|8.5|in|cm|}} diameter clutch.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' />

