===Exterior===
[[File:Jaguar XJ220 - Flickr - Alexandre Prévot (3).jpg|thumb|right|Rear three-quarters view of the production XJ220]]

The exterior retained the aluminium body panels of the XJ220 concept, but for the production cars [[Abbey Panels]] of Coventry were contracted to manufacture the body shells.<ref name='JaguarPress1992' /> The scissor doors were dropped for the production model and significant redesign work was carried out on the design when the wheelbase and overall length of the car was altered. [[Geoff Lawson (designer)|Geoff Lawson]], design director at Jaguar at the time, took a greater interest in the car and insisted the design had to be seen as a Jaguar if it were to be successful in promoting the company.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=51}} Keith Helfet returned to undertake the necessary redesign work mandated by the change in the wheelbase, which was reduced by {{cvt|200|mm|in|1}}.<ref name='PH1' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=52}}
The turbocharged engine required larger air intakes to feed the two intercoolers. Situated between the doors and the rear wheels, the air intakes were larger on the production version of the XJ220 than on the concept car. A number of small design changes for the body were tested in the wind tunnel; the final version had a [[drag coefficient]] of {{cd|0.36}} with downforce of {{cvt|3000|lb|kg|0|order=flip}} at {{cvt|200|mph||0}}.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=52}}{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=51}} The XJ220 was one of the first road cars to intentionally use underbody airflow and the [[venturi effect]] to generate downforce.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=51}} Additionally, the XJ220 could be ordered with optional [[BBS Kraftfahrzeugtechnik|BBS]] wheels for improved brake ventilation and aesthetics.

The rear lights used on the production XJ220 were sourced from the [[Rover 200 / 25#Rover 200|Rover 200]].<ref name='EvoLights'>{{cite news |first=Stephen |last=Dobie |title=Jaguar XJ220 tuned by Overdrive AD |date=25 January 2013 |publisher=[[Dennis Publishing]] |url=http://www.evo.co.uk/news/evonews/287815/jaguar_xj220_tuned_by_overdrive_ad.html |work=[[Evo (magazine)|Evo]] |access-date=26 June 2013 |archive-date=28 January 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130128041358/http://www.evo.co.uk/news/evonews/287815/jaguar_xj220_tuned_by_overdrive_ad.html? |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Chassis===
The production model utilised the same [[Alcan]] bonded honeycomb aluminium structure vehicle technology (ASVT) as the concept car for the chassis.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=56}} The chassis design featured two box section rails which acted as the suspension mounting points and would provide an [[crumple zone|energy absorbing structure]] in the event of a frontal impact; these were successfully tested at speeds up to {{cvt|30|mph}}. An integral [[roll cage]] formed part of the chassis and [[monocoque]], providing additional structural rigidity for the car and allowing the XJ220 to easily pass stringent crash testing.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /><ref name='JaguarPress1992' />

The rear-wheel steering was dropped from the production car to save weight and reduce complexity, as was the height adjustable suspension and active aerodynamic technology. The suspension fitted to the production model consisted of front and rear [[independent suspension]], [[double wishbone suspension|double unequal length wishbones]], inboard [[coil spring]]s and [[anti-roll bar]]s, with Bilstein gas-filled [[shock absorber|dampers]].<ref name='JaguarPress1992' />

The braking system was designed by AP Racing and featured ventilated and cross-drilled discs of {{cvt|13|in|cm|1}} diameter at the front and {{cvt|11.8|in|cm|1}} diameter at the rear.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /><ref name='JaguarPR' /> The calipers were four-piston aluminium units. JaguarSport designed the handbrake, which has separate calipers acting on the rear brake discs. Feedback from enthusiasts and racing drivers resulted in the decision to drop the [[anti-lock braking system]] from the production car.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=47}} The braking system was installed without a servo, but a number of owners found the brakes to be difficult to operate when cold and subsequently requested a servo to be fitted.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=88}}

A rack and pinion steering was fitted, with 2.5 turns lock to lock;<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /><ref name='OctaneSpec' /> the steering had [[Power steering|power assistance]].<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /> The [[Bridgestone]] Expedia S.01 asymmetric uni-directional tyres were specially developed for the XJ220.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /> Rally alloy wheel specialists Speedline Corse designed the alloy wheels, these are both wider and have a larger diameter on the rear wheels; {{cvt|17|in|cm|1}} wheels are fitted to the front and {{cvt|18|in|cm|1}} are fitted at the rear, with 255/55 ZR17 tyres at the front and 345/35 ZR18 tyres at the rear.<ref name='JaguarInfoSheet' /><ref name='JaguarPR' />{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=87}}

===Interior===
[[File:Jaguar XJ220 1993 Cockpit LakeMirrorClassic 17Oct09 (14577501306).jpg|thumb|left|Interior]]

The interior was designed for two passengers and trimmed in leather. Leather trimmed sports seats were fitted together with electric windows and electrically adjustable heated mirrors.<ref name='XJ220Order' /> The dashboard unusually curves round and carries onto the drivers door, with a secondary instrument binnacle containing four analogue gauges, including a clock and voltmeter fitted on the front of the drivers door. Air conditioning and green tinted glazing was also fitted.<ref name='XJ220Order' />

The luggage space consists of a small [[Trunk (automobile)|boot]] directly behind and above the rear portion of the engine, also trimmed in leather.<ref name='XJ220Order' />

===Production===
The car was assembled in a purpose-built factory at Wykham Mill, [[Bloxham]] near [[Banbury]] in [[Oxfordshire]].<ref name='BBCTG1' /> [[Diana, Princess of Wales]] officially opened the factory and unveiled the first production XJ220 in October, 1991.<ref name='JaguarTour'>{{cite news |title=Jaguar XJ220 Flying Visit Tour&nbsp;– Model History |year=1992 |publisher=[[Jaguar Cars]] and RTA PR |url=http://www.jag-lovers.org/brochures/220_92.html |work=Jaguar XJ220 Flying Visit Tour |access-date=25 June 2013 |archive-date=4 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130704220752/http://www.jag-lovers.org/brochures/220_92.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name='CCB1'>{{cite news |title=Jaguar XJ220 Anniversary Reunion |date=6 June 2012 |url=http://www.classic-car-buyer.co.uk/news/news-archive/1162-jaguar-xj220-anniversary-reunion |work=Classic Car Buyer |access-date=25 June 2013 |archive-date=21 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150621105432/http://www.classic-car-buyer.co.uk/news/news-archive/1162-jaguar-xj220-anniversary-reunion |url-status=live }}</ref>

The JV6 engines used in the Jaguar racing cars were produced by Swiss engineer Max Heidegger, but delivering the number of engines required for the XJ220 program was considered beyond his capacity.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=56}} TWR formed a division, TWR Road Engines, to manage the design, development, construction and testing of the engines for the production cars.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=56}}{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=87}} The JV6 engine used in the XJ220 featured little commonality with the engines Heidegger built for use in the XJR racing cars, being specifically engineered to meet performance and in particular, the European emissions requirements, which the race engines didn't have to meet.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=57}}{{sfnp|Porter|1994|p=127}}

FF Developments, in addition to the design work undertaken on the gearbox and rear axle assembly, was given responsibility for the manufacture of these components.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=57}}{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=87}} The aluminium chassis components and body panels were manufactured and assembled at the Abbey Panels factory in Coventry, before the [[body in white]] was delivered to the assembly plant at Bloxham.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=85}} The car, including chassis and body components, consists of approximately 3,000 unique parts.{{sfnp|Moreton|2010|p=82}}

The first customer delivery occurred in June 1992 and production rates averaged one car per day.<ref name='JaguarPR' /> The last XJ220 rolled off the production line in April 1994; the factory was then transferred to [[Aston Martin]] and used for the assembly of the [[Aston Martin DB7]] until 2004.<ref name='AMDB7-1'>{{cite web |url=http://www.astonmartin.com/the-company/history |title=Aston Martin Lagonda Ltd&nbsp;– Company History |access-date=27 June 2013 |publisher=[[Aston Martin]] |archive-date=25 February 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140225062127/http://www.astonmartin.com/the-company/history |url-status=dead }}</ref>{{efn|Jaguar and Aston Martin came under the control of the [[Ford Motor Company]] in 1994.<ref name='Autocar-XJ41'>{{cite news |first=Andrew |last=Frankel |title=Jaguar XJ41: The lost F-type |date=28 April 2013 |url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/concept-cars/jaguar-xj41-lost-f-type |work=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]] |access-date=27 June 2013 |archive-date=13 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130613072348/http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/concept-cars/jaguar-xj41-lost-f-type |url-status=live }}</ref>}}
{{clear}}

==Guinness World Record==

Jaguar had performed high speed testing of the pre-production XJ220 with chassis number 004 at the Firestone [[proving ground]] in [[Fort Stockton, Texas]] in 1991 and recorded a maximum speed of {{cvt|212.3|mph|kph}}, which was faster than any production car at the time.<ref name='JaguarPR' /><ref name="Car 1992" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Read |first=Keith |date=July 27, 1991 |title=Jag clocks up record |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/coventry-evening-telegraph-jag-clocks-up/179956643/ |work=[[Coventry Evening Telegraph]] |pages=4 |via=Newspapers.com}}</ref>

According to ''Car'' magazine, Jaguar hoped to attain {{cvt|220|mph|kph|0}} with the XJ220, mainly for promotional reasons. The decision was made to undertake further high speed testing in June 1992 at the [[Nardò Ring]] in Italy, with one journalist from ''[[Road & Track]]'' and one from the ''[[Car (magazine)|Car]]'' magazine together with a photographer and a BBC cameraman recording for [[Top Gear (1977 TV series)|BBC ''Top Gear'']] as independent witnesses. None of the independent witnesses understood how Jaguar's testing equipment worked. The pre-production XJ220 with chassis number 009 would be driven by [[Martin Brundle]], who had won the [[1990 24 Hours of Le Mans]] driving the [[Jaguar XJR-12|XJR-12]].<ref name="Car 1992" />

The initial high speed runs, with the car configured in standard trim (catalytic converters connected, lower rev-limit of 7,400&nbsp;rpm) achieved a maximum recorded speed of {{cvt|212.3|mph|kph}}, the same speed as previously reached at Fort Stockton during testing.{{efn|A lower speed of 211.2&nbsp;mph was recorded by a second on-board telemetry device}} Brundle reported that the car was hitting the rev limiter during the run.<ref name="Car 1992" />

The rev limiter was increased to 7,900&nbsp;rpm and the catalytic converters were removed in favour of installing straight-through exhaust pipes for a second series of runs, and it was this attempt which resulted in the maximum recorded speed of {{cvt|217.1|mph|kph}}.{{efn|A lower speed of 216&nbsp;mph was recorded by the second on-board telemetry device}} It was estimated that the removal of the catalytic converters, which were not mandated in Europe at the time, increased the power output by around {{cvt|51|PS|kW hp}}.<ref name="Car 1992" />

[[Guinness World Records|Guinness Book of World Records]] recorded the 217.1&nbsp;mph run as the official fastest speed ever attained by a standard production car, this figure was reported in the 1994 to 1999 editions of the Guinness Book of World Records. Guinness World Records then recognised the [[McLaren F1]] driven by [[Andy Wallace (racing driver)|Andy Wallace]] which achieved a maximum recorded speed of {{cvt|240.1|mph|kph}} in March 1998 (also with rev-limiter increased) as the fastest standard production car in the world.<ref name="Guinness1994">{{cite book |last1=Matthews |first1=Peter |title=The Guinness Book of World Records |edition=40 |location=London |publisher=Guinness World Records |year=1994 |page=118 |isbn=978-0851125121 }}</ref>
{{clear}}

==Reception and sales==
Press coverage of the XJ220 concept in 1988 was overwhelmingly positive and contributed to the decision in 1989 to put the XJ220 into limited production. The production version of the car was first shown to the public in October 1991, at the [[Tokyo Motor Show#1991|Tokyo Motor Show]].<ref name='MSN02'>{{cite news |first=Henry |last=Biggs |title=13 motoring disasters for Friday the 13th |date=13 October 2006 |publisher=Microsoft |url=http://cars.uk.msn.com/news/articles.aspx?cp-documentid=147862280 |work=MSN Cars |access-date=27 June 2013 }}{{Dead link|date=January 2020 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> The first car was launched for press review in Autumn 1991.<ref name='JaguarTour' />

''Autocar''{{'}}s Andrew Frankel was the first journalist to road-test the car and reported: "Savage acceleration really is a given here. What's really incredible about the XJ220 is its ability to provide such performance in a way that never, ever intimidates." He was particularly impressed with the throttle response, the driver's ability to control the performance of the car very precisely, and the way in which the engine delivers its power progressively rather than in one short burst.<ref name='Autocar-Quote'>{{cite news |first=Andrew |last=Frankel |title=Jaguar XJ220 |date=19 October 1992 |work=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]]}}</ref>{{page needed|date=July 2013}}

''[[Performance Car (magazine)|Performance Car]]'' reviewer John Barker was also impressed with the performance as well as the ride and stability of the car, writing "The V6 has a rumbly, loping note which, in league with a remarkably supple ride, belies the speed we are travelling at. I glance to the speedo and have trouble believing that it is indicating 170&nbsp;mph." Barker was also impressed with the engineering, saying "this car is catalysed, fully homologated and has passed the same tests that a Volvo needs before going on sale", going on to discuss how the vehicle looked at home on the racetrack thanks to the design.<ref name='PerformanceCar-Quote'>{{cite news |first=John |last=Barker |title=Jaguar XJ220 |date=October 1992 |work=[[Performance Car (magazine)|Performance Car]]}}</ref> ''Autocar''{{'}}s verdict was "Right now, the XJ220 gives us a standard by which all other fast cars can be compared. For the few who will actually own and, hopefully, use their XJ220s, the fact that they are in command of the most accomplished supercar ever made should suffice."<ref name='Autocar-Quote' />

Ergonomics and ride were also praised by Gavin Green in ''[[Car (magazine)|Car]]'' August 1992: "you sit straight ahead, pedals and four-spoked Nardi wheel beautifully positioned. There's none of the askew nonsense that plagues Italian supercars...forward visibility is panoramic and side vision is good. Rear vision is better than on some other mid-engined monsters." He went on to describe the ride: "The XJ220 rides over knobbly tarmac with extraordinary dexterity...the big Jag rides urban blacktop better than many sports saloons, never mind supercars."<ref name="CARAug1992b">{{cite news |first=Gavin |last=Green |title=The last temptation|date=August 1992 |work=[[Car (magazine)|Car]]}}</ref>

In a comparison test published in the ''[[Car (magazine)|Car]]'' March 1994 issue, the testers liked the "sheer blistering pace, looks and a superb cabin" but its size, the doors not opening far enough and handling were criticised: "If there's a more evil device on our roads, I wouldn't like to find it, for the XJ220 suffers from immense initial [[understeer]] followed by violent and snappy pendulous oversteer."
Most disappointing was the engine, at idle it sounded "like someone's clanking a bucket of rusty nails together". 
While its rival, the [[Bugatti EB 110]], impressed the testers, the XJ220 disappointed: "The Jaguar is outmoded and lacks soul: it looks like a cynical marketing exercise and feels it in its lack of purity and coherence."<ref name = "CarM94">{{cite news |first=Mark |last=Gillies |title=Blessed Things |date=March 1994 |work=[[Car (magazine)|Car]]}}</ref>

Motoring journalists have been critical of its size, being too big for a two-seater with virtually no luggage space, too wide to fit through traffic restrictors or to drive comfortably on most roads.<ref name="CARAug1992b"/> The very heavy unassisted steering and pedals,<ref name='AutoBild'>{{cite news|title=Das Superding|date=15 October 2010|url=http://www.autobild.de/klassik/artikel/jaguar-xj-220-von-1992-1255158.html|work=[[Auto Bild]]|access-date=5 September 2016|archive-date=24 June 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160624173655/http://www.autobild.de/klassik/artikel/jaguar-xj-220-von-1992-1255158.html|url-status=live}}</ref> underwhelming brakes without ABS, poor directional stability<ref name='AMS'>{{cite news |title=Jaguar XJ 220, der riesige Flop |date=25 February 2014 |url=http://www.auto-motor-und-sport.de/news/auto-der-woche-jaguar-xj220-der-riesige-flop-8086769.html |work=[[Auto, Motor und Sport]] |access-date=5 September 2016 |archive-date=15 September 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160915065644/http://www.auto-motor-und-sport.de/news/auto-der-woche-jaguar-xj220-der-riesige-flop-8086769.html |url-status=live }}</ref> and "terrible visibility"<ref name="ACJun93">[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]] June 1993 p.34–43</ref> were also disliked.
Most criticised was the behavior at low revs, the engine sound was described with words like "a pail of nuts and bolts being poured through a Magimix",<ref name="ACJun93"/> rattling clutch, grinding transmission, crackling chassis, rumbling and groaning body contributing to the impression of imminent breakdown. Driving in the city was sheer torture, worsened by the first gear being far too tall for stop-and-go traffic.<ref name='AMS'/>

Sales performance was disappointing. Jaguar had intended to produce up to 350 cars, but production ceased in 1994 with 281 production cars produced, not all of which had been sold; some left-hand drive examples were still available in 1997.<ref name='Unsold1'>{{cite web |url=http://www.targafloriocars.com/Car_Sales/JaguarXJ220/4512.htm |title=Jaguar XJ220 |access-date=27 June 2013 |work=Targa Florio Cars |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130629091140/http://www.targafloriocars.com/Car_Sales/JaguarXJ220/4512.htm |archive-date=29 June 2013 }}</ref><ref name='Indy1'>{{cite news |first=James |last=Ruppert |title=Motoring: Burnt fingers for hot car speculators |date=6 December 1997 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/business/motoring-burnt-fingers-for-hot-car-speculators-1287076.html |work=[[The Independent]] |access-date=27 June 2013 |archive-date=5 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305032226/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/business/motoring-burnt-fingers-for-hot-car-speculators-1287076.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The [[Early 1990s recession|recession]] combined with the drastic changes to the production version left many of those who placed a deposit unable to complete the purchase. The index linking of contracts exacerbated the issue, and added almost £200,000 to the purchase price between early 1990 and mid-1992.<ref name='MSN01'>{{cite news |first=Sean |last=Carson |title=Jaguar XJ220: An endangered species |date=7 February 2012 |publisher= [[Microsoft]] |work=MSN Cars }}</ref> The [[McLaren F1]] suffered from similarly poor sales performance, with just 71 cars sold against McLaren's target of 300. McLaren's F1 program eventually turned a small profit due to the sale and servicing of the 28 GTR racing variants produced.<ref name='Rowan1'>{{cite news |first=Rowan |last=Atkinson |author-link=Rowan Atkinson |title=Rowan Atkinson decides: Bugatti Veyron or McLaren F1? |date=August 2011 |url=http://www.classicandperformancecar.com/features/octane_features/271015/rowan_atkinson_decides_bugatti_veyron_or_mclaren_f1.html |work=[[Octane (magazine)|Octane]] |access-date=12 July 2013 |archive-date=8 May 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130508071509/http://www.classicandperformancecar.com/features/octane_features/271015/rowan_atkinson_decides_bugatti_veyron_or_mclaren_f1.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Jaguar customers attempting to withdraw from their contracted purchases were given the option to buy themselves out of their contracts, but by 1995, the issue had resulted in legal action as buyers claimed the specification changes rendered any contracts void. Jaguar produced evidence clearly demonstrating that the vehicle specification shown in the contract matched the vehicle that was delivered and the presiding judge, [[John Donaldson, Baron Donaldson of Lymington|John Donaldson]], quickly ruled in Jaguar's favour.<ref name='XJ220Order' /><ref name='Indy1' /> The last of the unsold XJ220s were sold for £127,550 plus VAT in 1997.<ref name='MSN02' /><ref name='Indy1' /> While never officially approved for sale in the United States, the XJ220 was approved under the [[Show or Display]] exemption by 2001.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://autoweek.com/article/car-news/display-speed-under-show-or-display-exemption-americans-can-now-import-previously|title=Display of Speed: Under the "Show or Display exemption, Americans can now import previously forbidden exotics|last=Vettraino|first=J.T.|date=7 January 2001|work=[[Autoweek]]|access-date=2014-10-17|archive-date=22 October 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141022151834/http://autoweek.com/article/car-news/display-speed-under-show-or-display-exemption-americans-can-now-import-previously|url-status=live}}</ref>

The XJ220 appeared in later motoring press articles; ''[[Evo (magazine)|Evo]]'' journalist David Vivian, writing a head-to-head test between the XJ220 and the [[Lamborghini Murciélago]] in 2009, commented that "going ludicrously fast seems trivially easy" and acknowledged that the decision to change the V12 engine for a turbocharged V6 engine "would garner more acceptance now". The Lamborghini delivered the far better driving experience however: "After the XJ220, it’s almost absurdly agile", "the big difference, and it is massive, is how much more exciting, entertaining and accessible the LP640 is to drive."<ref name='Evo3'>{{cite news |first=David |last=Vivian |title=Lamborghini Murcielago LP640 v Jaguar XJ220 |date=June 2009 |url=http://www.evo.co.uk/carreviews/cargrouptests/235489/lamborghini_murcielago_lp640_v_jaguar_xj220.html |work=[[Evo (magazine)|Evo]] |access-date=24 June 2013 |archive-date=6 October 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131006194657/http://www.evo.co.uk/carreviews/cargrouptests/235489/lamborghini_murcielago_lp640_v_jaguar_xj220.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
{{clear}}

==Racing==
[[File:Jaguar XJ220S Front JagMENA.jpg|thumb|Jaguar XJ220S]]
A racing version was introduced at the 1993 [[Autosport International]] motor show; given the model name XJ220-C, it was built to compete in [[Fédération Internationale du Sport Automobile|FISA]] GT racing.<ref name='XJ220CBrochure'>{{cite news |title=TWR Racing&nbsp;– Engineering Excellence XJ220-C |year=1993 |publisher=[[Tom Walkinshaw Racing]] }}</ref> The XJ220-C driven by [[Win Percy]] won its first race, a round of the [[British GT Championship|BRDC National Sports GT Challenge]] at [[Silverstone Circuit|Silverstone]].

Three works XJ220-Cs were entered in the [[1993 24 Hours of Le Mans]] race, in the newly created [[Grand tourer|Grand Touring]] Class. [[John Nielsen (racing driver)|John Nielsen]], [[David Brabham]] and [[David Coulthard]] won the GT class, beating Porsche by two laps; the other two cars retired, both through engine failure. However, the class win was revoked when the Jaguar XJ220-C was controversially disqualified for failing to run with [[catalytic converter]]s.<ref name='RedBullDC'>{{cite web |url=http://www.redbull.com/cs/Satellite/en_INT/Article/David-Coulthard-021243130938337 |title=David Coulthard |access-date=26 June 2013 |work=Redbull.com |publisher=[[Red Bull GmbH]] |archive-date=25 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201125060607/https://www.redbull.com/cs/Satellite/en_INT/Article/David-Coulthard-021243130938337 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name='ESPNDC'>{{cite news |title=David Coulthard |year=2011 |url=http://www.espn.co.uk/mclaren/motorsport/driver/1171.html |publisher=[[ESPN]] |access-date=26 June 2013 |archive-date=23 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240323134758/https://www.espn.com/f1/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name='BBCTG-Racing'>{{cite news |first=Richard |last=Hammond |author-link=Richard Hammond |title=Richard Hammond drives the icons |date=May 2012 |url=http://www.topgear.com/uk/photos/richard-hammond-drives-the-icons-jaguar-XJ220-2012-05-24 |work=[[Top Gear (magazine)|Top Gear Magazine]] |access-date=26 June 2013 |archive-date=15 August 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120815120239/http://www.topgear.com/uk/photos/richard-hammond-drives-the-icons-jaguar-xj220-2012-05-24 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref name='1993LeMansResults'>{{cite web |url=http://www.monoli.com/24h/classements/1993.htm |title=Classement des 24 heures du Mans 1993 |access-date=26 June 2013 |year=1993 |work=61ème Edition des 24 Heures du Mans&nbsp;– 70 ans |publisher=Automobile Club de l'Ouest |language=fr, en |archive-date=6 September 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110906083708/http://www.monoli.com/24h/classements/1993.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> The Jaguars had passed scrutiny and completed the first day of qualifying when senior steward Alain Bertaut complained that Jaguar were not running catalytic converters. The cars had been entered under the [[IMSA GT Championship|IMSA GT]] category and Bertaut claimed that they needed to run with catalysts.<ref name='COYS1' /> The cars ran in the race under appeal. [[International Motor Sports Association]] (IMSA) officials wrote to the [[Automobile Club de l'Ouest]] (ACO) (English: Automobile Club of the West), organisers of the [[24 Hours of Le Mans]], confirming that the XJ220-Cs entered had complied with IMSA rules. Jaguar won their appeal (supported by the FIA) but were nevertheless disqualified, as the ACO confirmed that the appeal had not been lodged in time.<ref name='COYS1' />

Four cars were entered in the GT1 class for the [[1995 24 Hours of Le Mans]], two by PC Automotive Jaguar and two by Chamberlain Engineering, though the latter did not run their cars. Neither team had Jaguar or TWR backing; both of PC Automotive's cars were outpaced by the new [[McLaren F1 GTR]].<ref name='1995LeMansResults'>{{cite web |url=http://www.monoli.com/24h/classements/1995.htm |title=Classement des 24 heures du Mans 1995 |access-date=26 June 2013 |year=1995 |work=63ème Edition des 24 Heures du Mans |publisher=Automobile Club de l'Ouest |language=fr, en |archive-date=6 September 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110906133123/http://www.monoli.com/24h/classements/1995.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> Richard Piper, [[Tiff Needell]] and James Weaver were holding fourth position until an engine failure during the night, ending their race, whilst the second XJ220-C retired after leaving the road.<ref name='1995LeMansResults' /><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.racingsportscars.com/photo/Le_Mans-1995-06-18.html |title=Le Mans 24 Hours 1995&nbsp;– Photo Gallery |publisher=Racing Sports Cars |date=18 June 1995 |access-date=20 January 2013 |archive-date=26 September 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120926002937/http://www.racingsportscars.com/photo/Le_Mans-1995-06-18.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Jaguar XJ220 GT at Goodwood 2014 004.jpg|thumb|{{center|Jaguar XJ220 GT, raced in the Italian GT championship}}]]

A lightly modified road-going XJ220 was also entered in the [[Italian GT Championship]] in 1993, although without factory support; where it raced in [[Martini (vermouth)|Martini]] livery.<ref name='Autocar-Martini'>{{cite news |title=Martini Racing icons: picture special |date=23 June 2013 |url=http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/goodwood-festival-speed/martini-racing-icons-picture-special |work=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]] |access-date=26 June 2013 |archive-date=26 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130626084219/http://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/goodwood-festival-speed/martini-racing-icons-picture-special |url-status=live }}</ref> This entry predated the factory's Le Mans entry.

The XJ220-C was promoted in the United States in the-made-for-TV "[[Fast Masters]]" racing series at [[Indianapolis Raceway Park]], airing on ESPN in the summer of 1993 and featuring invited drivers over 50 years old in an elimination format.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1993/07/04/sports/auto-racing-jaguar-takes-lumps-with-goosebumps.html|title=Jaguar Takes Lumps With Goosebumps|last=Siano|first=Joseph|date=4 July 1993|work=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=17 October 2014|archive-date=27 October 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141027091823/http://www.nytimes.com/1993/07/04/sports/auto-racing-jaguar-takes-lumps-with-goosebumps.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The competition became a farce due to the cars' viscous [[Limited-slip differential]]s proving ill-suited for the oval track the organizers had chosen, producing strange handling characteristics that resulted in several crashes.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Perkins|first=Chris|date=2018-12-27|title=Fast Masters Was a Demolition Derby Featuring Jaguar XJ220s|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/motorsports/a25694196/fast-masters-history-jaguar-xj220c/|access-date=2021-01-10|website=Road & Track|language=en-US|archive-date=12 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210112073914/https://www.roadandtrack.com/motorsports/a25694196/fast-masters-history-jaguar-xj220c/|url-status=live}}</ref>

TWR developed a further six road cars called the XJ220-S, featuring one-piece [[carbon-fibre-reinforced polymer]] front and rear bodywork; the engine was tuned to {{cvt|700|PS|kW hp|0}}. The XJ220-S models did away with the [[hidden headlamp]]s of the original and instead opted for perspex covered lights. The S models were essentially road-going versions of the XJ220-C race car and as a result featured a much simpler race-orientated interior with kevlar seats and the removal of the leather trim.<ref name='JaguarPR' /><ref name='PH-S'>{{cite news |first=Matt |last=Rigby |title=SPOTTED: JAGUAR XJ220S |date=17 January 2012 |publisher=[[Haymarket Media Group]] |url=http://www.pistonheads.com/news/default.asp?storyId=25028.htm |work=[[PistonHeads]] |access-date=26 June 2013 |archive-date=23 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240323134804/https://www.pistonheads.com/regulars/ph-spottedykywt/spotted-jaguar-xj220s/25028 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Colin Goodwin]], a writer for ''[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]]'', tested an XJ220-S in June 1995 at [[General Motors Proving Grounds#Millbrook Proving Ground|Millbrook Proving Ground]] and set the lap record at an average speed of {{cvt|180.4|mph|kph|}}.<ref name='220S-Speed'>{{cite news |title=Meryl and Bob on track for love; Falling in Love (BBC1, 11.50pm&nbsp;– 1.40am). |date=13 March 1999 |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Meryl+and+Bob+on+track+for+love%3B+Falling+In+Love+%28BBC1,+11.50pm+-...-a060431853 |work=[[Daily Record (Scotland)|Scottish Daily Record]] |access-date=26 June 2013 |archive-date=12 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180712025846/https://www.thefreelibrary.com/Meryl+and+Bob+on+track+for+love;+Falling+In+Love+(BBC1,+11.50pm+-...-a060431853 |url-status=live }}</ref>
{{clear}}

==Jaguar XJ220 Pininfarina==
The Jaguar XJ220 Pininfarina is a special XJ220 built in 1995 for the [[Hassanal Bolkiah|Sultan of Brunei]] and his brother [[Jefri Bolkiah, Prince of Brunei|Prince Jefri]], who commissioned a number of rare and one-off heavily modified cars based on expensive luxury cars.<ref name="wsj2">{{cite news |last=Maremont |first=Mark |author-link=Mark Maremont |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB120433378645304553 |title=Will the Prince Turn Pauper? Billions Later, Jefri's in a Royal Mess |date=1 March 2008 |access-date=27 June 2013 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150512023904/https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB120433378645304553 |archive-date=12 May 2015 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription}}</ref> This car was modified by [[Pininfarina]] with the redesigned body being manufactured by coach maker [[Sergio Coggiola|Coggiola]] with modifications including fixed headlights, new rear lights with a redesigned double-vane rear wing, and a new interior package. The car also comes with dark green exterior paint.<ref name='Pininfarina1'>{{cite web |url=http://www.supercars.net/cars/3596.html |title=1995 Jaguar XJ220 Pininfarina |access-date=27 June 2013 |last=Owen |first=Richard |work=Supercars.net |archive-date=23 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240323134948/https://www.supercars.net/blog/1995-jaguar-xj220-pininfarina/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name='Pininfarina2'>{{cite web |url=http://www.automoblog.net/2006/10/09/jaguar-xj220-pininfarina/ |title=Jaguar XJ220 Pininfarina |access-date=27 June 2013 |last=Burdick |first=Chris |date=9 October 2006 |publisher=Automoblog.net |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130511181756/http://www.automoblog.net/2006/10/09/jaguar-xj220-pininfarina/ |archive-date=11 May 2013 }}</ref>

